# RISC-V Privileged Architecture – CSR, Exceptions and Traps

## Goal

The goal of this project is to extend the existing RV32IMA processor with the basic mechanisms required by the RISC-V Privileged Architecture.

The focus is on:

- Control and Status Registers (CSRs)
- Privileged processor state
- Exceptions
- Traps
- Trap entry and return
- Basic supervisor/machine-mode concepts
- Connecting the exception and trap system to the existing pipeline

The project aims to implement the subset required to make the processor capable of handling synchronous exceptions and basic traps in a structured way.

The existing RV32IMA instruction set remains unchanged.

---

## Why This Is Needed

The previous processor implementation mainly focused on executing normal instructions.

For a real operating-system-capable processor, this is not enough.

The processor must also be able to react to events such as:

- Illegal instructions
- Misaligned memory accesses
- Instruction access faults
- Load/store access faults
- System calls
- Interrupts

These events cause the processor to enter a trap handler.

CSRs provide the processor with the registers needed to store information about the trap and control privileged execution.

This is an important step toward eventually running more advanced software and an operating system.

---

# 1. RISC-V Privileged Architecture

RISC-V separates normal application execution from privileged execution using privilege modes.

The main modes defined by RISC-V are:

- User mode ("U")
- Supervisor mode ("S")
- Machine mode ("M")

For this project, Machine mode is the primary focus.

Machine mode is the highest privilege level and is present on all standard RISC-V implementations.

Supervisor mode may be added later if required by the Linux target.

---

## Current Privilege Level

The processor needs to keep track of its current privilege mode.

For example:

M-mode
  |
  | trap
  v
Trap Handler

Initially, the processor can start in Machine mode.

The privilege mode affects which operations and CSRs are accessible.

---

# 2. Control and Status Registers (CSRs)

CSRs are special processor registers used to control and monitor the CPU.

They are accessed using CSR instructions rather than normal load/store instructions.

Important CSR instructions include:

CSRRW
CSRRS
CSRRC
CSRRWI
CSRRSI
CSRRCI

These instructions allow software to:

- Read CSRs
- Write CSRs
- Set bits
- Clear bits

---

# 3. Important CSRs

The first implementation focuses on the Machine-mode CSRs required for trap handling.

### "mstatus"

Contains machine-mode status information.

Important fields include:

 "MIE" – Machine Interrupt Enable
 "MPIE" – Previous Machine Interrupt Enable
 "MPP" – Previous Privilege Mode

These fields are used when entering and leaving traps.

---

### "mtvec"

Machine Trap-Vector Base Address.

It specifies where trap handling begins.

Example:


mtvec = 0x00000100


When a trap occurs:


PC ← mtvec


The exact trap-vector behavior depends on the configured mode.

---

### "mepc"

Machine Exception Program Counter.

This stores the address of the instruction that caused the trap, or the appropriate exception PC defined by the architecture.

Example:


mepc = PC_of_faulting_instruction


It allows the trap handler to determine where execution came from.

---

### "mcause"

Contains the reason for the trap.

It distinguishes between Exceptions and Interrupts and contains a cause code.

Conceptually:


mcause
+----------------+----------------+
| Interrupt flag |   Cause code   |
+----------------+----------------+


---

### "mtval"

Contains additional information about certain traps.

Depending on the trap type, it can contain information such as:

- A faulting address
- An instruction related to the exception
- Zero for traps where no additional value is required

---

### "mie"

Machine Interrupt Enable.

This CSR controls which machine-level interrupts are enabled.

---

### "mip"

Machine Interrupt Pending.

This CSR represents pending machine-level interrupts.

---

# 4. CSR Addressing

CSRs have 12-bit addresses.

Example conceptual mapping:


CSR address
    |
    v
+---------+
| CSR File|
+---------+
    |
    +--> mstatus
    +--> mtvec
    +--> mepc
    +--> mcause
    +--> mtval
    +--> mie
    +--> mip


The CSR unit should provide:


csr_addr
csr_write_data
csr_read_data
csr_write_enable


The CPU can then access CSRs through the existing instruction pipeline.

---

# 5. CSR Instructions

CSR instructions operate differently from normal arithmetic instructions.

For example:

asm
csrrw x1, mtvec, x2


Conceptually:

x1 ← mtvec
mtvec ← x2


Another example:

asm
csrrs x1, mstatus, x2


Conceptually:

x1 ← mstatus
mstatus ← mstatus OR x2


The CSR unit therefore needs to support:

- Read
- Write
- Set
- Clear
- Immediate variants

---

# 6. Exceptions

An exception is a synchronous event caused by the currently executing instruction.

Examples include:

- Illegal instruction
- Instruction address misalignment
- Instruction access fault
- Load address misalignment
- Load access fault
- Store/AMO address misalignment
- Store/AMO access fault
- Environment call ("ECALL")
- Breakpoint ("EBREAK")

The exact set implemented initially depends on which conditions the current processor and memory system can detect.

---

# 7. System Instructions

The processor needs to recognize privileged/system instructions.

Important instructions include:

- ECALL
- EBREAK
- MRET
- WFI

For this project, the most important instructions are:

### ECALL

Used by software to intentionally request an environment call.

For example:
- asm
- ecall

This causes a synchronous exception.

---

### EBREAK

Used to generate a breakpoint exception.

- asm
- ebreak

This can later be useful for debugging.

---

### MRET

Returns from a Machine-mode trap handler.

ex:
- asm
- mret

Conceptually:

PC ← mepc
Privilege ← previous privilege
MIE ← MPIE
MPIE ← 1

The exact state transition follows the RISC-V Privileged Architecture specification.

---

# 8. Trap Entry

When an exception occurs, the processor must save the required state and transfer control to the trap handler.

A simplified trap entry sequence is:


Exception detected
       |
       v
Save PC → mepc
       |
       v
Save cause → mcause
       |
       v
Save additional value → mtval
       |
       v
Update mstatus
       |
       v
PC ← mtvec
       |
       v
Execute trap handler


The original instruction flow is interrupted.

---

# 9. Trap Return

The trap handler eventually executes:

- asm
- mret

This restores the required machine state and returns execution to the saved program counter.

Conceptually:

Trap Handler
     |
     | MRET
     v
Restore privilege state
     |
     v
PC ← mepc
     |
     v
Continue execution

This mechanism is essential for operating-system and firmware-level software.

---

# 10. Pipeline Integration

The processor already uses a 5-stage pipeline:

IF → ID → EX → MEM → WB

Exceptions therefore need to be handled together with pipeline control.

A simplified example:


IF      ID      EX      MEM     WB
        ADD     ECALL
                ^
                |
             Exception

When an exception is detected, younger instructions already present in the pipeline must not be allowed to modify architectural state.

The pipeline therefore needs a mechanism to:

- Detect exceptions
- Cancel invalid instructions
- Prevent incorrect register writes
- Prevent incorrect memory writes
- Save the correct PC
- Redirect execution to "mtvec"

---

# 11. Trap Flush

A trap requires the pipeline to be flushed.

Conceptually:

Normal execution:

IF → ID → EX → MEM → WB

Trap detected
      |
      v
Flush younger instructions
      |
      v
PC ← mtvec
      |
      v
Fetch trap handler

This is similar to branch flushing, but trap handling also requires CSR state updates.

---

# 12. Exception Detection

Exception detection should be connected to the appropriate pipeline stages.

For example:

| Exception                | Detection stage |
| ------------------------ | --------------- |
| Illegal instruction      | ID              |
| ECALL                    | ID              |
| EBREAK                   | ID              |
| Instruction misalignment | IF              |
| Instruction access fault | IF              |
| Load misalignment        | MEM             |
| Load access fault        | MEM             |
| Store misalignment       | MEM             |
| Store access fault       | MEM             |

The exact implementation depends on the existing memory and instruction-fetch architecture.

---

# 13. Trap Priority

Multiple events can potentially occur during the same cycle.

The processor therefore needs a defined priority system.

For example:


Highest priority
       |
       v
Instruction fetch exceptions
       |
       v
Instruction decode exceptions
       |
       v
Memory exceptions
       |
       v
Interrupts
       |
       v
Normal execution


The exact priority must follow the RISC-V architecture where applicable rather than being arbitrarily chosen.

---

# 14. CSR Unit Architecture

A possible implementation is:


             +----------------+
             |   CSR Unit     |
             +----------------+
                |    |    |
                |    |    +--> mtval
                |    +-------> mcause
                +-----------> mepc
                     |
                     +-------> mstatus
                     +-------> mtvec
                     +-------> mie
                     +-------> mip


The CSR unit receives:

- csr_addr
- csr_write_data
- csr_write_enable
- csr_operation

and produces:

- csr_read_data

Trap logic can also directly update the required CSRs.
---

# 15. Trap Controller

A dedicated trap controller can simplify the implementation.

Example:


             +----------------+
             |  Trap Sources  |
             +----------------+
               |    |    |
               v    v    v
            Illegal ECALL Memory
               \    |    /
                \   |   /
                 v  v  v
              +---------+
              |  Trap   |
              | Control |
              +---------+
                   |
          +--------+--------+
          |        |        |
          v        v        v
        mepc     mcause   mtval
                   |
                   v
                 mtvec
                   |
                   v
                  PC


This keeps trap handling separate from the normal ALU datapath.

---

# 16. Interrupts vs Exceptions

The processor must distinguish between exceptions and interrupts.

### Exception

Synchronous with the currently executing instruction.

Examples:

- Illegal instruction
- ECALL
- Misaligned access

### Interrupt

Asynchronous relative to the currently executing instruction.

Examples include:

- Timer interrupt
- External interrupt
- Software interrupt

This project primarily establishes the trap infrastructure so that interrupts can later use the same mechanism.

---

# 17. Relation to the Existing Interrupt Controller

The existing SoC contains an interrupt controller.

The new trap system provides the CPU-side mechanism required to handle interrupts.

The architecture can eventually become:

Timer
  |
  v
Interrupt Controller
  |
  v
CPU interrupt input
  |
  v
Trap Controller
  |
  +--> mcause
  +--> mepc
  +--> mstatus
  |
  v
mtvec
  |
  v
Interrupt Handler

This connects the previosly implemented SoC interrupt system with the processor's privileged architecture.

